ntity and business registration (look up CAC in Nigeria), match invoice to PO and contract, verify bank account details through a trusted channel (not just email), check for currency and exchange rate consistency, ensure compliance with Nigerian tax laws (VAT, WHT), check for any sanctions or anti-money laundering concerns, look for spelling errors, domain email mismatches, pressure tactics. I'll also suggest a call with the supplier on a known number to confirm. This is a practical question, so I'll provide a clear, organized answer.
00:06:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: When an invoice is flagged—especially from an overseas supplier—it’s a sign to do a deeper review, not automatically assume fraud. The goal is to make sure you’re paying the right entity, for legitimate goods or services, into a verified bank account. Below is a structured checklist of what to look for, with specific considerations for a Nigerian supplier given common financial crime typologies in the region (business email compromise, mandate fraud, VAT scams, etc.). ### 1. Validate the Supplier’s Identity & Business Legitimacy - **Check the company registration** in Nigeria’s Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC) database. Ask the supplier for their **RC Number** (Registration Certificate number) and verify it online at the CAC portal. Confirm the company name, status (active/inactive), and directors match what you know. - **Verify physical address and contact details.** Cross-check the address on the invoice with independent sources (website, contract, Google Maps). Call the supplier using a known phone number—not the one on the invoice if it’s new or changed. - **Request references** or past transaction history if this is a new supplier. ### 2. Verify the Invoice Against Your Purchase Order/Contract - Does the **description of goods/services** match what was ordered? Look for vague line items like “consultancy fees” or “processing charges” that weren’t in the original agreement. - Are **quantities, unit prices, and totals** consistent with the PO or contract? Recalculate manually. - Check the **currency and exchange rate**. If the invoice is in Naira (NGN) but your contract is in USD, confirm the applied rate is reasonable for the invoice date and wasn’t manipulated. ### 3. Scrutinize Bank Account Details (This Is Critical) This is the most common point of fraud. Criminals intercept emails and swap bank account details. - **Never rely solely on the invoice** for bank information. Cross-check the bank account name, number, and sort/IBAN against a **trusted source**: the bank details provided at supplier onboarding, a recent known-good invoice paid successfully, or directly confirmed by phone/video call with a known contact. - **Watch for last-minute changes.** If you receive an “updated bank account” email, treat it as a red flag. Verify the change using a second channel (e.g., call the procurement manager on a number you already have, not one from the change request). - In Nigeria, be extra cautious if the **account name doesn’t perfectly match** the registered company name, or if the bank is in a different country (e.g., a UK or US account for a Nigerian supplier, unless explicitly agreed). Ask why. - Confirm the **bank’s legitimacy**—ensure it’s a licensed Nigerian bank listed on the Central Bank of Nigeria’s website. ### 4. Tax and Compliance Checks (Nigerian-Specific) - **Tax Identification Number (TIN):** Nigerian suppliers should have a TIN issued by the Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS). Ask for a copy of the TIN certificate and verify it on the FIRS portal if possible. - **Withholding Tax (WHT):** Many payments to Nigerian suppliers attract withholding tax (e.g., 5% or 10% depending on the nature of the transaction). The invoice should clearly separate any WHT deductions or show the gross amount with a note about tax liability. Some fraudulent invoices omit this to pocket the tax portion. If you’re meant to deduct and remit, ensure the net payment is correct. - **Value Added Tax (VAT):** Valid Nigerian VAT is 7.5%. Check that the supplier is VAT-registered (they should have a VAT registration number) and that the VAT amount on the invoice is correctly calculated. A missing or inflated VAT line is suspicious. - **Customs/duties:** If goods were imported, check for a Pre-Arrival Assessment Report (PAAR) and Form M, which should predate the invoice. ### 5. Red Flags in Communication and Documentation - **Email domain mismatch:** If the supplier usually uses @legitcompany.com but the invoice comes from @legit-company.com.ng or a free email like Gmail/Yahoo, halt. - **Pressure tactics:** Urgency (“payment needed today to avoid shipment delay”) without a valid reason. - **Poor quality logos, spelling errors, inconsistent formatting** compared to previous invoices. - **Invoice number sequence:** If this is a first-time invoice, that’s normal. If it’s a repeat supplier, check if the invoice number follows the typical pattern. Gaps or uncharacteristic numbers could indicate a fake. - **Beneficiary name vs. company name:** Sometimes fraudsters register a company with a name very similar to your real supplier (e.g., “ABC Nigeria Ltd” vs. “ABC Nigera Ltd”). Compare character by character. ### 6. Internal Process & Due Diligence - **Segregation of duties:** Ensure the person who sets up the supplier is not the same person approving the invoice and releasing payment. - **Supplier onboarding file:** Review what due diligence was done initially. For a Nigerian supplier, this should ideally include a bank confirmation letter, a certificate of incorporation, a board resolution authorizing the signatory, and a utility bill for the registered address. - **Sanctions and adverse media screening:** Check the company, its directors, and the bank account IBAN/Routing against sanctions lists (OFAC, UN, EU) and for any negative news (fraud allegations, court cases). This can be done via free resources or a compliance software. ### 7. Confirm the Transaction Context - **Is the transaction logical?** Does the amount, frequency, and purpose align with your business relationship? A sudden large invoice for “consulting” when you usually buy raw materials warrants extra scrutiny. - **Check your own records:** Has this supplier been paid recently? Was that payment received and acknowledged? Sometimes fraudsters send a duplicate invoice. ### 8. Direct Verification (The Golden Step) After doing all the above, pick up the phone (or do a video call) to a trusted contact at the supplier—not using contact details from the flagged invoice. Verbally confirm: - The invoice number, amount, and due date. - The bank account name and number you intend to pay. - Any recent changes to their payment process. Document this verification in your payment approval file. --- **If anything doesn’t add up, stop the payment process immediately.** Loop in your legal and compliance teams. For a Nigerian supplier, you might also consider engaging a local legal firm or using a commercial escrow service for large first-time payments, but that’s a separate decision. A methodical check like this protects your company without assuming bad faith—it’s standard practice for any high-value or flagged foreign invoice. Stay professional and transparent with the supplier: a genuine vendor will understand and cooperate with reasonable verification requests.
